555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1497 0 R
/E (The actual database used in this project contains records of traffic accidents in the United States, with detailed information on geolocation, time, weather conditions, and road infrastructure. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2 3]
>>
endobj
556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1498 0 R
/E (In total, the dataset includes 500,000 samples and 46 variables. This is a reduced version chosen for computational feasibility. The goal of the study is to predict the severity of an accident and identify the key factors that most influence the likelihood of a serious incident. The target variable is represented as a binary indicator - Severity_bin. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1499 0 R
/E (From a management perspective, understanding the determinants of serious accidents allows authorities and transport services to optimize traffic safety measures, prioritize infrastructure investments, and improve emergency response. After describing and preparing the data, including handling missing values, forming weather conditions and time groups, removing redundant variables \(which correlate with the basic ones\), and balancing classes, Random Forest and H2O Gradient Boosting models were implemented for the classification task.)
/Pg 10 0 R
>>
endobj
558 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 464 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Overview)
/E (Overview)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
560 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1500 0 R
/E (We decided to compare multiple models for this analysis. We started with a Random Forest model with 100 trees, in 3 different variations. The original Random Forest showed good results and an acceptable recall, but was lackluster in precision and feature interpretability, while strongly correlating to others \(like longitude and latitude\). After modifying the original in two different versions, one where we decided to exclude accident coordinates, the other where balancing was applied. Results varied somewhat, for example when balancing precipitations became a major factor and without coordinates more economically interpretable features gained importance \(crossings, roundabouts, etc.\). Regarding AUC and recall though, the best model was the simplest, the original random forest. This brought us to the next step-)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
561 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1501 0 R
/E (Implementing Gradient Boosting where the focus shifted towards model optimization compared to data cleanign and feature selectio. In the boosting model features where selected with the help of loops and the overall results where marginally superior to Random Forest. A pattern was emerging though, state seemed to be the most dominant feature in correlation. This leads us to our final step-)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
562 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1502 0 R
/E (A Multi-Layer Perceptron model where we played to its strength and simplified the dataset to exclude all but two states and compare the two. The results were unfortunately worse in regards to recall and AUC but nonetheless it served as valuable information in regards to managerial implications, demonstrating that state response is the most significant yet still relatively small part of what comprises the severity of an accident. Not everything can be managerially accounted for and controlled.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
