/K [6]
>>
endobj
740 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Basic Model serves as our primary Random Forest specification. It achieves an AUC of 0.867 and an accuracy of 0.789, making it the strongest performer among the three. This version uses the full feature set produced after data preparation, without any additional balancing or variable reductions. Its superior results indicate that Random Forest benefits from having access to the complete set of geographic, temporal, and weather-related predictors. The model effectively captures severity patterns in the national dataset and provides a stable benchmark for evaluating alternative configurations.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
741 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
742 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (OVERALL ASSESSMENT)
/E (OVERALL ASSESSMENT)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
743 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (BALANCED RANDOM FOREST)
/E (BALANCED RANDOM FOREST)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
744 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The comparison shows that the Basic Random Forest remains the preferred specification, offering the best balance between accuracy, class-ranking ability \(AUC\), and model stability. The Balanced Model is useful when prioritizing detection of severe accidents, but the added noise reduces its overall predictive power. In contrast, the Reduced-Variables Model demonstrates that removing correlated predictors weakens the model substantially, reinforcing the importance of retaining the full feature set for this task.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
745 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
746 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Balanced Model, created by increasing the representation of severe accidents using SMOTE and row sampling, achieves an AUC of 0.833 and accuracy of 0.749. Although weaker than the Basic Model, it performs substantially better than the Reduced-Variables version. The improvement in sensitivity \(visible in the confusion matrix\) reflects the benefits of rebalancing in identifying rare severe cases. However, this gain comes at the cost of slightly lower overall accuracy and ranking ability, as oversampling increases noise in the training distribution.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
747 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Comparison of Variable Importance Across Specifications)
/E (Comparison of Variable Importance Across Specifications)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
748 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To understand how the Random Forest model responds to different data and feature configurations, we compare the variable importance rankings of the three specifications: Basic, Balanced, and Reduced-Variables. Although all three share the same underlying algorithm, their importance patterns shift depending on feature availability and sampling strategy. These differences help identify which predictors are structurally important and which change when the model setup is altered. The Basic Random Forest, which is our main specification, consistently identifies the same core set of predictors as most influential. State is by far the dominant variable, followed by Distance\(mi\), Wind_Direction, Traffic_Signal, Precipitation_missing, Crossing, Station, and the geographic coordinates Start_Lat and Start_Lng. Time-related features such as Start_Time_hour and Start_Time_dayOfWeek also appear, but with lower impact. Because this model uses the full feature set, it captures the geographic, infrastructural, and weather-related patterns that drive accident severity across the United States. This specification produces the most stable and interpretable results and therefore serves as the preferred model.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
749 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
750 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Comparison of Variable Importance Across Specifications)
/E (Comparison of Variable Importance Across Specifications)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
751 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Reduced-Variables Random Forest, which removes correlated predictors, shows a clear decline in model quality and a narrowing of variable importance. State remains the most influential variable, followed by Distance\(mi\), but many weather and geographic variables drop sharply because their correlated counterparts have been removed. Variables such as Traffic_Signal, Crossing, Wind_Speed, and Station appear artificially more important simply because competing features are no longer available. This confirms that removing correlated predictors strips away useful information, since Random Forest models are naturally robust to multicollinearity.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
752 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Overall, across all three specifications, State and Distance\(mi\) consistently emerge as the most important predictors, highlighting the strong geographic structure underlying accident severity. The Basic Model produces the most balanced and meaningful importance distribution, while the Balanced and Reduced models reveal how sensitive Random Forest can be to data composition and feature richness. These results reinforce the choice of the Basic Random Forest as the main specification for interpretation and comparison in this project.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
